  "comparisonMode": "projected-observable",
  "orderingPolicy": "strict-projected-equality",
  "orderingNote": "Strict equality of every projected observable. Two normalisations are disclosed rather than implied: MCP call-arrival order is unordered within a single concurrent tool batch (calls issued from different assistant turns stay ordered), and the persisted system/context entry position is compared as counted presence rather than exact position.",
  "allowDocumentedDifferences": false,
  "comparisonNote": "Compares externally observable behavior only: provider request message sequences, provider-visible tool calls/results, agent lifecycle events, queue update events, steering/follow-up counts, response ordering, cancellation/abort outcome, tool result/error projection, session entries and active branch state, persisted results, MCP side effects, and settlement. Every ordered observable is compared as observed: effects, lifecycle events and declared tool lists are never sorted, and there is no known-difference allowance. The one exception is the arrival order of MCP calls issued from a single assistant turn: both implementations dispatch those concurrently (pinned Pi maps over the call array and awaits Promise.all), and pinned Pi was measured producing both arrival orders for the same case, so that observable is compared as a multiset while the set of calls is still required to match. The system/context entry position relative to the first user prompt is a session-format detail compared as counted presence (system xN) rather than exact position. assistant is_error is projected from the explicit pisharp.isError flag (Pi records provider failure via stopReason, not assistant isError).",
  "summary": {
